{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "Di8kfLCYerNJ"
   },
   "source": [
    "# Vaccine RCT Examples\n",
    "\n",
    "We use vaccine trial counts to illustrate estimation of risk differences and vaccine efficacy, then compare several confidence interval methods. Reported risks and efficacy estimates are proportions unless explicitly expressed as percentages."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "fW54aax9mE2G",
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "r"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "install.packages(\"PropCIs\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "r"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "library(PropCIs)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "Id8Df7PUerNX"
   },
   "source": [
    "# Polio RCT\n",
    "\n",
    "In the randomized portion of the 1954 Salk polio vaccine trial, children received either the vaccine or a placebo. Participants and the doctors assessing outcomes did not know the assignments. The vaccine group had 33 polio cases among 200,745 children; the placebo group had 115 among 201,229.\n",
    "\n",
    "We estimate the average treatment effect as the difference in case proportions. The estimate is about $-40.7$ cases per 100,000 children, with an approximate 95% confidence interval of $[-52.6,-28.9]$. Under the RCT assumptions, the difference estimates the causal effect of vaccination on the risk of polio.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the outcomes are Bernoulli, the group totals and case counts suffice to calculate the difference in means and its estimated standard error. We also estimate vaccine efficacy,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\mathsf{VE}=1-\\frac{\\text{Risk for Vaccinated}}{\\text{Risk for Unvaccinated}},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "which measures the relative reduction in risk. The point estimate is approximately 71.2%.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the risk difference, we use a normal confidence interval. For vaccine efficacy, we simulate independent Gaussian draws for the two estimated risks using their estimated variances, calculate efficacy for each draw, and take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. This is an approximate simulation-based interval, not an exact interval."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "_cell_guid": "b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19",
    "_uuid": "8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5",
    "id": "SE8nvAWberNc",
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "r"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "NV <- 200745 # number of vaccinated (treated)\n",
    "NU <- 201229 # number of unvaccinated (control)\n",
    "RV <- 33 / NV # average outcome for vaccinated\n",
    "RU <- 115 / NU # average outcome for unvaccinated\n",
    "VE <- (RU - RV) / RU\n",
    "# vaccine efficacy\n",
    "\n",
    "# incidence per 100000\n",
    "incidence_rv <- RV * 100000\n",
    "incidence_ru <- RU * 100000\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Incidence per 100000 among treated:\", round(incidence_rv, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Incidence per 100000 among controlled:\", round(incidence_ru, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "# treatment effect - estimated reduction in incidence per 100000 people\n",
    "delta_hat <- 100000 * (RV - RU)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Estimated ATE of occurances per 100,000 is\", round(delta_hat, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "# variance, standard deviation and confidence interval of ATE using that outcomes are Bernoulli\n",
    "var_rv <- RV * (1 - RV) / NV\n",
    "var_ru <- RU * (1 - RU) / NU\n",
    "var_delta_hat <- 100000^2 * (var_rv + var_ru)\n",
    "std_delta_hat <- sqrt(var_delta_hat)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Standard deviation for ATE is\", round(std_delta_hat, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "ci_delta <- c(\n",
    "  delta_hat - 1.96 * sqrt(var_delta_hat),\n",
    "  delta_hat + 1.96 * sqrt(var_delta_hat)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\n",
    "  \"95% confidence interval of ATE is [\", round(ci_delta[1], 4), \",\",\n",
    "  round(ci_delta[2], 4), \"]\"\n",
    "))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Overall VE is\", round(VE, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "# we use an approximate bootstrap to find the confidence interval of vaccine efficacy\n",
    "# via Monte Carlo draws\n",
    "set.seed(1)\n",
    "B <- 10000 # number of bootstraps\n",
    "RVs <- RV + rnorm(B) * sqrt(var_rv)\n",
    "RUs <- RU + rnorm(B) * sqrt(var_ru)\n",
    "VEs <- (RUs - RVs) / RUs\n",
    "\n",
    "plot(density(VEs), col = 2, main = \"Approximate Distribution of VE estimates\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ci_ve <- quantile(VEs, c(.025, .975)) # use the empirical quantiles from the bootstraps\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\n",
    "  \"95% confidence interval of VE is [\", round(ci_ve[1], 4), \",\",\n",
    "  round(ci_ve[2], 4), \"]\"\n",
    "))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "ahcRWgyEerNk"
   },
   "source": [
    "# Pfizer/BioNTech COVID-19 RCT\n",
    "\n",
    "The [FDA briefing](https://www.fda.gov/media/144245/download) reports results from a randomized trial comparing a two-dose vaccine regimen with placebo. Participants and the observers assessing outcomes were blinded to assignment. The table below reports vaccine efficacy against symptomatic, confirmed COVID-19, overall and by age group.\n",
    "\n",
    "![Pfizer/BioNTech trial efficacy results](https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/oiO6gYom1UZyrOhgpFx2iq8ike979u3805JHiVygP-Efh1Yaz2ttyPcgWKlT1AqHDM4v46th3EPIkOvRLyXA0fNUloPL-mL9eOFmSAzfbNOHyCZSQ0DyzMhcFUtQuZ520R5Qd2lj)\n",
    "\n",
    "For a simple illustration of the two-means approach, we treat the reported case counts and group totals as binomial data, abstracting from differences in follow-up. The calculations illustrate inference methods rather than reproduce the trial analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "We first apply the normal and Gaussian simulation approaches used in the polio example. We then pool the 65–74 and 75-and-older groups to illustrate how interval estimates differ when there are few observed cases."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "_cell_guid": "79c7e3d0-c299-4dcb-8224-4455121ee9b0",
    "_uuid": "d629ff2d2480ee46fbb7e2d37f6b5fab8052498a",
    "id": "mdrjpK4XerNl",
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "r"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "NV <- 19965\n",
    "# number vaccinated\n",
    "NU <- 20172\n",
    "# number unvaccinated\n",
    "RV <- 9 / NV\n",
    "# average outcome for vaccinated\n",
    "RU <- 169 / NU\n",
    "# average outcome for unvaccinated\n",
    "VE <- (RU - RV) / RU\n",
    "# vaccine efficacy\n",
    "\n",
    "# incidence per 100000\n",
    "incidence_rv <- RV * 100000\n",
    "incidence_ru <- RU * 100000\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Incidence per 100000 among vaccinated:\", round(incidence_rv, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Incidence per 100000 among unvaccinated:\", round(incidence_ru, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "# treatment effect - estimated reduction in incidence per 100000 people\n",
    "delta_hat <- 100000 * (RV - RU)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Estimated ATE of occurances per 100,000 is\", round(delta_hat, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "# variance, standard deviation and confidence interval of ATE using that outcomes are Bernoulli\n",
    "var_rv <- RV * (1 - RV) / NV\n",
    "var_ru <- RU * (1 - RU) / NU\n",
    "var_delta_hat <- 100000^2 * (var_rv + var_ru)\n",
    "std_delta_hat <- sqrt(var_delta_hat)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Standard deviation for ATE is\", round(std_delta_hat, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "ci_delta <- c(\n",
    "  delta_hat - 1.96 * sqrt(var_delta_hat),\n",
    "  delta_hat + 1.96 * sqrt(var_delta_hat)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\n",
    "  \"95% confidence interval of ATE is [\", round(ci_delta[1], 4), \",\",\n",
    "  round(ci_delta[2], 4), \"]\"\n",
    "))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"Overall VE is\", round(VE, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "# we use an approximate bootstrap to find the VE confidence interval\n",
    "# using Monte Carlo draws as before\n",
    "set.seed(1)\n",
    "B <- 10000\n",
    "RVs <- RV + rnorm(B) * sqrt(var_rv)\n",
    "RUs <- RU + rnorm(B) * sqrt(var_ru)\n",
    "VEs <- (RUs - RVs) / RUs\n",
    "\n",
    "plot(density(VEs), col = 2, main = \"Approximate Distribution of VE estimates\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ci_ve <- quantile(VEs, c(.025, .975))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\n",
    "  \"95% confidence interval of VE is [\", round(ci_ve[1], 4), \",\",\n",
    "  round(ci_ve[2], 4), \"]\"\n",
    "))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "kqupG5u7erNn"
   },
   "source": [
    "## Participants Aged 65 and Older\n",
    "\n",
    "Pooling the two older age groups gives 1 case among 4,044 vaccinated participants and 19 among 4,067 placebo participants. Estimated efficacy is approximately 94.7%. We repeat the Gaussian simulation to construct two-sided intervals and one-sided lower confidence bounds. With so few cases, the approximation can produce negative simulated risks and efficacy values above 100%."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "kOcfliFTerNo",
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "r"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Here we calculate the overall effectiveness of the vaccine for the two groups that are 65 or older\n",
    "NV <- 3239 + 805\n",
    "NU <- 3255 + 812\n",
    "RV <- 1 / NV\n",
    "RU <- (14 + 5) / NU\n",
    "VE <- (RU - RV) / RU\n",
    "print(paste(\"Overall VE is\", round(VE, 4)))\n",
    "\n",
    "var_rv <- RV * (1 - RV) / NV\n",
    "var_ru <- RU * (1 - RU) / NU\n",
    "\n",
    "# As before, we use an approximate bootstrap to find the confidence intervals\n",
    "# using Monte Carlo draws\n",
    "\n",
    "set.seed(1)\n",
    "B <- 10000\n",
    "RVs <- RV + rnorm(B) * sqrt(var_rv) + 10^(-10)\n",
    "RUs <- RU + rnorm(B) * sqrt(var_ru) + 10^(-10)\n",
    "VEs <- (RUs - RVs) / RUs\n",
    "\n",
    "plot(density(VEs), col = 2, main = \"Approximate Distribution of VE estimates\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ci_ve <- quantile(VEs, c(.025, .975))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\n",
    "  \"two-sided 95 % confidence interval is [\", ci_ve[1], \",\",\n",
    "  ci_ve[2], \"]\"\n",
    "))\n",
    "\n",
    "one_sided_ci_ve <- quantile(VEs, c(.05))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\n",
    "  \"one-sided 95 % confidence interval is [\", one_sided_ci_ve[1], \",\",\n",
    "  1, \"]\"\n",
    "))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "A8wEW0sQerNq"
   },
   "source": [
    "## Binomial Parametric Bootstrap\n",
    "\n",
    "We next simulate case counts from binomial distributions with the observed group sizes and estimated risks. The cell uses the ratio of simulated counts as an approximation to the risk ratio because the group sizes are nearly equal. The resulting percentile intervals remain approximate; simulating binomial counts does not make a bootstrap interval exact."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "3WTthWWeerNr",
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "r"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "NV <- 3239 + 805\n",
    "NU <- 3255 + 812\n",
    "RV <- 1 / NV\n",
    "RU <- (14 + 5) / NU\n",
    "VE <- (RU - RV) / RU\n",
    "print(paste(\"Overall VE is\", VE))\n",
    "\n",
    "set.seed(1)\n",
    "B <- 10000 # number of simulation draw\n",
    "RVs <- rbinom(100000, size = NV, prob = RV)\n",
    "RUs <- rbinom(100000, size = NU, prob = RU)\n",
    "VEs <- (RUs - RVs) / RUs\n",
    "\n",
    "plot(density(VEs), col = 2, main = \"Approximate Distribution of VE estimates\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ci_ve <- quantile(VEs, c(.025, .975))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\n",
    "  \"two-sided 95 % confidence interval is [\", ci_ve[1], \",\",\n",
    "  ci_ve[2], \"]\"\n",
    "))\n",
    "\n",
    "one_sided_ci_ve <- quantile(VEs, c(.05))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(paste(\"one-sided 95 % confidence interval is [\", one_sided_ci_ve[1], \",\", 1, \"]\"))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "MjenLiQAerNw"
   },
   "source": [
    "# Score-Based Confidence Intervals for the Risk Ratio\n",
    "\n",
    "The next cells use `PropCIs::riskscoreci` to construct an approximate score confidence interval for the risk ratio. Transforming its endpoints from $[L,U]$ to $[1-U,1-L]$ gives an interval for vaccine efficacy. This is a score-based approximation, not the exact Cornfield procedure introduced below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "XwFMp7vLerNz",
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "r"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Exact CI exploiting Bernoulli outcome using the Cornfield Procedure\n",
    "NV <- 19965\n",
    "NU <- 20172\n",
    "RV <- 9 / NV\n",
    "RU <- 169 / NU\n",
    "VE <- (RU - RV) / RU\n",
    "\n",
    "1 - riskscoreci(9, NV, 169, NU, 0.95)$conf.int[2]\n",
    "1 - riskscoreci(9, NV, 169, NU, 0.95)$conf.int[1]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "jQ64-QaxerN0"
   },
   "source": [
    "The overall score-based efficacy interval is approximately **[89.6%, 97.2%]**. It is close to, but does not exactly reproduce, the interval in the FDA table."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "iQ_lO8userN2"
   },
   "source": [
    "We repeat the score-based calculation for participants aged 65 and older. The interval is approximately **[68.9%, 99.1%]**. With only one vaccinated case and 19 placebo cases, the choice of interval method has a substantial effect on the lower endpoint."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "iP0ZCUw8erN3",
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "r"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Exact CI exploiting Bernoulli outcome for the two groups that are 65 or older\n",
    "NV <- 3239 + 805\n",
    "NU <- 3255 + 812\n",
    "RV <- 1 / NV\n",
    "RU <- (14 + 5) / NU\n",
    "VE <- (RU - RV) / RU\n",
    "\n",
    "1 - riskscoreci(1, NV, 19, NU, 0.95)$conf.int[2]\n",
    "1 - riskscoreci(1, NV, 19, NU, 0.95)$conf.int[1]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "cornfield-method-description"
   },
   "source": [
    "# Cornfield Exact Conditional Interval\n",
    "\n",
    "We now compute the Cornfield exact conditional confidence interval for the odds ratio; see [Cornfield (1956), *A Statistical Problem Arising from Retrospective Studies*](https://digicoll.lib.berkeley.edu/record/112867).\n",
    "\n",
    "For these rare outcomes, the odds ratio approximates the risk ratio. If $[L,U]$ is the odds-ratio interval, we therefore use $[1-U,1-L]$ as an approximate interval for vaccine efficacy. The conditional interval is exact for the odds ratio; its interpretation as an efficacy interval uses the rare-outcome approximation.\n",
    "\n",
    "The following cell calculates intervals for the full sample and for participants aged 65 and older."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": [
    "cornfield_ve_ci <- function(cases_v, n_v, cases_u, n_u) {\n",
    "  tab <- matrix(\n",
    "    c(cases_v, n_v - cases_v,\n",
    "      cases_u, n_u - cases_u),\n",
    "    nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE\n",
    "  )\n",
    "\n",
    "  ci_or <- fisher.test(tab, conf.level = 0.95)$conf.int\n",
    "\n",
    "  # Approximate VE interval: odds ratio approximates risk ratio\n",
    "  c(lower = 1 - ci_or[2], upper = 1 - ci_or[1])\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Overall\n",
    "cornfield_ve_ci(9, 19965, 169, 20172)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Ages 65 and older\n",
    "cornfield_ve_ci(1, 3239 + 805, 19, 3255 + 812)"
   ],
   "metadata": {
    "id": "pvs4wwrbLpdF"
   },
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "cornfield-results-description"
   },
   "source": [
    "The Cornfield calculations give approximate efficacy intervals of **[89.6%, 97.6%] overall** and **[66.8%, 99.9%] for participants aged 65 and older**. The wider interval for older participants reflects the limited information in the small case counts. Differences across interval methods are particularly noticeable in that group."
   ]
  }
 ],
